body:
## What and why

Closes #95. Results > Plots > Path Plot now samples a typed or surface-picked polyline, draws its value-versus-distance graph and viewport station markers, and exports `distance,x,y,z,value` CSV. `results.path` is the same read-only typed session command through the desktop, CLI, scripts and MCP. Outside stations become null values and plot/CSV gaps; there is no nearest-node substitution.

The pure FEM locator supports all six existing solid families: Tet4, Tet10, Wedge6, Pyramid5, Hex8 and Hex20. It uses the actual isoparametric shape functions, bounded inverse solves and conservative outward-rounded Bernstein/control bounds for curved geometry. An exhausted inverse search reports unresolved instead of silently classifying a potentially inside point as outside. Actual-node Jacobians are checked, including a folded Tet10 regression; the Pyramid5 apex has its explicit singular-chart handling. This establishes local query admissibility, not global injectivity of arbitrary folded meshes.

The engine caches a deterministic element BVH per exact current result, with the lowest element label winning shared-face ties. Storage is SI; points, distances, scalar replies and CSV convert once through Quantity. All five display presets and Celsius/Kelvin/Fahrenheit are covered. Categorical contact status is refused; numerical contact quantities interpolate only defined contributing values, ignoring exactly-zero shape coefficients without renormalizing.

Picking uses genuine rendered triangle hits and barycentric natural coordinates, followed by the full FE map to recover reference coordinates even on curved/deformed displays. Draft points retain their SI precision through all display presets and actual widget rendering. Opening the command again preserves an unsampled draft; fresh drafts inherit compatible visible modes/frames. The plot labels retain their own step/field/frame/mode when the active result view changes. Graph decimation preserves interior extrema in station order and conservatively breaks bins containing gaps; graph scaling preserves both multiplicative unit changes and temperature offsets.

Sampling and export run as cancellable background jobs, with index-building and station-loop progress checks. Cancellation, stale results and failed exports preserve the previous successful plot. Programmatic calls never open a dialog. Index storage is capped at 500,000 elements, two million result nodes and 96 MiB; construction has an independent 14-million basis-evaluation budget, queries four million, with bounded candidates and BVH visits. The guide documents the caps and numerical scope; original demonstration recipes are included below. Numerical-method source citations remain next to the implementation. Generated feature documentation is updated; ROADMAP is unchanged.

## Current review changes

The locator cites Sederberg only through the official BYU archive, with its recorded January 10, 2012 publication metadata. The two demonstration recipe files and one-run write-up have moved out of the repository into this description. Three existing regression tests now define their synthetic models directly, retaining the analytic conductor, transient-frame inheritance and actual curved/deformed surface picking checks. Rebasing onto upstream develop retains both the new orphan import and path command modules. No production interpolation or UI behavior changed.

Linux CPU validation at `b4e3e1840749adefc34ca6847aa0fc00049f4555` (rebased on `e8ee48d0`):

- FEM:96 passed; engine:395 passed with15 heavy tests ignored; UI:204 passed plus3 doctests. The full engine run found only stale generated feature totals; these are corrected to88 ready/34 coming soon, and the exact catalogue/page guard passed on rerun. No engine tests were excluded on Linux.
- Scoped `cargo check`, all-target clippy with warnings denied, formatting, manual simplification review and diff checks passed. The source tree is unchanged by autosquash into the original3 logical commits.
- Builds retained the cached dependency-feature resolution; only FEM,engine and UI test binaries were executed. This revision ran no GPU numerical/native capture, wasm build or full workspace gate. Historical attachments below retain their original source revision and provenance.

## Historical demonstration at 0b324482

Windows native evidence was captured from a dedicated build of the exact tested head `0b32448242f693680985eb8e67b88bb78490b061`. CPU solvers were used with an actual NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 Vulkan viewport. The 24-second H.264 video is a captioned sequence of six genuine presented app frames, not reconstructed UI or an offscreen rendering. It shows beam stress, actual deformed curved-surface picking, the sampled endpoint graph, steady conduction temperature and outside gaps. Two stills and a small numerical verification JSON accompany it. Older private `5e3b302` layout captures are superseded and are not part of this handoff.

The coordinator normal-pushed all four immutable originals through ordinary Git in artifact-queue commit `9889db110eaacab5573cabcaecf6219540daa48d`, under `objects/results/path-plots-95/0b32448242f693680985eb8e67b88bb78490b061/`: `solid-path-plots.mp4`, `curved-surface-path.png`, `temperature-path-gaps.png` and `native-verification.json`. Original and staged blob SHA-256 values and byte counts match; the full video decodes successfully. Maintainer receipts confirm all four artifacts received, with SHA-256 values and byte counts matching the immutable originals. The maintainer has attached all three native media files at the URLs below. The JSON is numerical evidence in the inbox, not a native attachment. No demonstration media or binaries are in the feature branch. The video is not a timing benchmark, physical transient, coupled two-fluid exchanger demonstration or experimental validation.

